    "q-335361": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335361",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=1+2x-y+z,\\, & y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=-2+x+2y,\\, & z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=x+y-z+2w,\\, & w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$, $z$ and $w$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1+2x-y+z \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot 0-0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-2+x+2y \\\\  & =-2+0+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =x+y-z+2w \\\\  & =0+0-1+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ |  | $1$ | $-2$ |  | $1$ | $1$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+2=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-2)=-1 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈2,$\n$z(1)≈-1,$\nand\n$w(1)≈2.$",
