    },
    "q-335316": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335316",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2x+3y-w,\\, & y(0)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=1-y+2z,\\, & z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=z-x,\\, & w(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈$\n{{field-6}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=-1,$ $z(0)=1,$ and $w(0)=0,$ we place $y=-1,$ $z=1,$ and $w=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+3y-w \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1)-0 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =1-y+2z \\\\  & =1-(-1)+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =z-x \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |  | $1$ | $4$ |  | $0$ | $1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-1)=-2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $-2$ |  |  | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈-2,$\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈\\frac{7}{3},$\nand\n$w\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈\\frac{1}{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
